<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-logarithm-properties" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Logarithm Properties</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
			<li><p>.</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>


	<subsection>
    <title>Laws of Logarithms</title>


    <p>
      Three formulas involving logarithms are collectively called the Laws of Logarithms:
    </p>

    <p>
      <principle xml:id="laws-of-logarithms">
        <title>Laws of Logarithms</title>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\log_b(MN) = \log_b(M) + \log_b(N)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\log_b(\frac{M}{N}) = \log_b(M) - \log_b(N)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\log_b(M^p) = p \cdot \log_b(M)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </principle>
    </p>
    <p>
      <warning>
        <p>
          <m>\log_b(M) + \log_b(N) \not= \log_b(M + N)</m>. Instead, <m>\log_b(M) + \log_b(N) = \log_b(MN)</m>.
        </p>
      </warning>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the log laws to expand the logarithms as much as possible: <m>\log_6\left(\frac{A^5B^3}{C^4D^2}\right)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_6(A^5) + \log_6(B^3) - \log_6(C^4) - \log_6(D^2)</m>
            <m>= 5\log_6(A) + 3\log_6(B) - 4\log_6(C) - 2\log_6(D)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the log laws to expand the logarithms as much as possible: <m>\ln(\frac{A}{B^2C^5})</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Recall that <m>\ln(z) = \log_e(z)</m>, so the laws of logarithms apply.  We have:
            <m>\ln\left(\frac{A}{B^2C^5}\right) = \ln(A) - \ln(B^{2}C^{5}) = \ln(A) - \left(2 \ln(B) + 5 \ln(C)\right) = \ln(A) - 2\ln(B) - 5\ln(C)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the log laws to expand the logarithms as much as possible: <m>\ln(\sqrt[3]{\frac{A}{B^2C^5}})</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            
            <m>\ln\sqrt[3]{\frac{A}{B^2C^5}} = \ln\left(\frac{A^{1/3}}{B^{2/3}C^{5/3}}\right) = \ln(A^{1/3}) - \ln(B^{2/3}C^{5/3}) = \frac{1}{3}\ln(A) - \left(\frac{2}{3}\ln(B) + \frac{5}{3}\ln(C)\right)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the log laws to expand the logarithms as much as possible: <m>\ln\sqrt{\frac{x+9}{3x+1}}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\ln\sqrt{\frac{x+9}{3x+1}} = \ln\left(\frac{(x+9)^{1/2}}{(3x+1)^{1/2}}\right) = \ln((x+9)^{1/2}) - \ln((3x+1)^{1/2}) = \frac{1}{2}\ln(x+9) - \frac{1}{2}\ln(3x+1)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite <m>\log_3(x) + \log_3(y) - 7\log_3(z)</m> as one logarithm
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_3(x) + \log_3(y) - 7\log_3(z) = \log_3(xy) - \log_3(z^7) = \log_3\left(\frac{xy}{z^7}\right)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite <m>\ln(x) + \ln(y) - 7\ln(z)</m> as one logarithm
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\ln(x) + \ln(y) - 7\ln(z) = \ln(xy) - \ln(z^7) = \ln\left(\frac{xy}{z^7}\right)</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>


  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Solving equations using Laws of Logarithms</title>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the equation <m>\log_2(x-6) + \log_2(x-3) = \log_2(4)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/>, we saw that if <m>\log_b(c)=\log_b(d)</m>, then <m>c=d</m>. 
          We cannot use this property right away because the left side of the equation is not a single logarithm.
        </p>
        <p>
          We can use the Laws of Logarithms to combine the two logarithms on the left side into one logarithm:
          <me>\log_2((x-6)(x-3)) = \log_2(4)</me>
          Now we can use the property from <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/> to set the inputs of the logs on each side equal to each other:
          <me>(x-6)(x-3) = 4</me>
          Expanding the left side and moving all terms to one side gives:
          <me>x^2 - 9x + 18 = 4</me>
          Subtracting <m>4</m> on both sides,
          <me>x^2 - 9x + 14 = 0</me>
          Factoring the left side gives:
          <me>(x-7)(x-2) = 0</me>
          Applying the Zero Product Property,
          <me>x-7=0 \text{ or } x-2=0</me>
          So
          <me>x=7 \text{ or } x=2</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since the original equation has logarithms, we must check for extraneous solutions based on <xref ref="principle-check-log-equation"/>.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Is <m>x=7</m> a solution? The input to the first logarithm is <m>7-6=1</m> and the input to the second logarithm is <m>7-3=4</m> and the input to the third logarithm is <m>4</m>. The inputs to all three logarithms are positive, so <m>x=7</m> is a solution.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Is <m>x=2</m> a solution? The input to the first logarithm is <m>2-6=-4</m> and the input to the second logarithm is <m>2-3=-1</m> and the input to the third logarithm is <m>4</m>. Because not all inputs are positive, <m>x=2</m> is not a solution.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>\ln(2) + \ln(x+6)=\ln(10)+\ln(x-1)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can use the Laws of Logarithms to rewrite the left side as <m>\ln(2(x+6))</m>.
          We can also use the Laws of Logarithms to rewrite the right side as <m>\ln(10(x-1))</m>.  So we have:
          <me>\ln(2(x+6)) = \ln(10(x-1))</me>
          Since we now have a single logarithm on each side (of the same base, namely <m>e</m>), we can use the property from <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/> to set the inputs of the logs on each side equal to each other:
          <me>2(x+6) = 10(x-1)</me>
          Expanding both sides gives:
          <me>2x + 12 = 10x - 10</me>
          Subtracting <m>2x</m> from both sides gives:
          <me>12 = 8x - 10</me>
          Adding <m>10</m> to both sides gives:
          <me>22 = 8x</me>
          Dividing both sides by <m>8</m> gives:
          <me>x = \frac{22}{8} = \frac{11}{4}</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Because the original equation had logarithms, we check to see if <m>x=\frac{11}{4}</m> is a solution.  
          The input to the first logarithm is <m>2</m>, the input to the second logarithm is <m>\frac{11}{4}+6 = \frac{35}{4}</m>, the input to the third logarithm is <m>10</m>, and the input to the fourth logarithm is <m>\frac{11}{4}-1 = \frac{7}{4}</m>.  
          All inputs are positive, so <m>x=\frac{11}{4}</m> is a solution.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>\ln(x-3) + \ln(x+1) = \ln(x+7)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can use the Laws of Logarithms to rewrite the left side as <m>\ln((x-3)(x+1))</m>.  So we have:
          <me>\ln((x-3)(x+1)) = \ln(x+7)</me>
          Since we now have a single logarithm on each side (of the same base, namely <m>e</m>), we can use the property from <xref ref="principle-solve-logarithmic-one-to-one"/> to set the inputs of the logs on each side equal to each other:
          <me>(x-3)(x+1) = x+7</me>
          Expanding the left side gives:
          <me>x^2 - 2x - 3 = x + 7</me>
          Subtracting <m>x + 7</m> from both sides gives:
          <me>x^2 - 3x - 10 = 0</me>
          Factoring the left side gives:
          <me>(x-5)(x+2) = 0</me>
          Applying the Zero Product Property,
          <me>x-5=0 \text{ or } x+2=0</me>
          So
          <me>x=5 \text{ or } x=-2</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We check to see if <m>x=5</m> is a solution.  The input to the first logarithm is <m>5-3=2</m>, the input to the second logarithm is <m>5+1=6</m>, and the input to the third logarithm is <m>5+7=12</m>.  All inputs are positive, so <m>x=5</m> is a solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          We check to see if <m>x=-2</m> is a solution.  The input to the first logarithm is <m>-2-3=-5</m>, the input to the second logarithm is <m>-2+1=-1</m>, and the input to the third logarithm is <m>-2+7=5</m>.  Not all inputs are positive, so <m>x=-2</m> is not a solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the only solution to the equation is <m>x=5</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <p>

      Less typical: <m>2^{3x+4} = 5^{2x-1}</m> or <m>3^{2x+1} = 7^{x-2}</m>
    </p>


  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Simplifying expressions</title>

    <principle xml:id="principle-log-exp-inverse-formulas">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>b^{\log_{b}(x)} = x</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\log_b(b^x) = x</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Simplify <m>5^{\log_5(k)}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Answer
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Simplify <m>5^{\log_5(k+6)}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Answer
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

